  "mathematics": {
    "fresh_checks": [
      "Modified comparison: positivity reserve, bounded ordered-contact sign outside the sublevel, bounded weighted Stokes passage, all five torsion coefficients and the I_k recurrence; coefficients at most n^2, q>7/8 and 1+q^(-1)+q^(-2)<4 close the induction, including n=1.",
      "Strict domination: choose epsilon before the sublevel height; strict-set positive volume and the retained epsilon^n smooth volume term give the contradiction even when the comparison measure vanishes. Bounded mass positivity and shifted exponential comparison follow with correct order.",
      "Thin-sublevel capacity: tau, comparison height and nested sublevels are justified; C_H=[2(H+1)]^n(8/7)^n is independent of epsilon, and every nonempty strict sublevel has positive capacity.",
      "Backward capacity iteration: check every new positive height before applying the estimate; the geometric sum remains below D and a fixed positive-height sublevel contradicts the halving capacity limit.",
      "Lp potential and density lower bounds: capacity-square control and global capacity tails produce the stated dependence and exclude zero density; no uniform lower mass over all bounded potentials is claimed.",
      "Uniform compactness: common C0 bound, positivity reserve and fixed height produce a positive sublevel-capacity lower bound; Chebyshev and Holder force the enlarged-sublevel mass to zero from L1 Cauchy convergence.",
      "Continuous scalar equation: root-density convergence gives a nonzero limiting Gauduchon trace, upper normalization bound and curve Stokes case; the density lower bound gives the reverse bound. Uniform convergence permits bounded-product identification.",
      "Exponential equation: absorb the fixed volume ratio, use the conserved Gauduchon trace and density-root probability in Jensen; normalized exponential integrability and Holder control the mean term, and the density lower bound controls additive constants from below.",
      "Density order, bounded-solution continuity and positive-exponent uniqueness: every comparison direction is checked, including vanishing densities; the auxiliary density exp(-u)f remains nonzero and in Lp.",
      "Vanishing exponent: constant translates of any scalar solution give both ordered barriers, uniform lambda u_lambda convergence to log c and scalar uniqueness. Normalized cluster potentials are precompact, without claiming their uniqueness.",
      "Classical scalar uniqueness: the extremum of u-(1+epsilon)v creates strict positivity of B; determinant monotonicity gives c<=(1+epsilon)^n, followed by the reversed estimate.",
      "Hopf equality: total mass is 6 c_f I_f and the fiber Poincare estimate has constant 4. Equality descends a continuous potential by local sections; invariance is first used as a measure identity, with Haar inversion and Fubini giving f=Af almost everywhere.",
      "Hopf converse: the closed projective base has mass one, hence k=I_f^(-1); bounded regularization and the vertical restriction beta=2 dA_E justify the cube coefficient 6 in local trivializations, then scalar uniqueness identifies c_f.",
      "Near equality: uniform potential compactness and reflexive weak Lp density compactness identify the equation and the base limit; contraction and self-adjointness of averaging yield both whole-sequence conclusions, with a fixed dual test for a failed weak limit.",
      "Oscillating Hopf densities: periodicity, strict positivity, rank expansion and all derivative-period averages give I_m=1+a^2/(2 pi^2 m^2). The potentials converge to their fiber average, but the density L1 defect tends to 2a/pi; contraction rules out strong approximation by any base pullbacks."
    ],
